{
  "_note": "Customer-facing jobs, defined once for every JLR model. A part is named by its callout BASE number - the middle of the engineer number (BJ32-2005-AC -> 2005), which JLR keeps from the Ford system and uses on every model. A callout matches its base exactly or with one trailing letter (19542 matches 19542A and 19542B); a list of bases matches any (oil filter: 6731 spin-on, 6714 cartridge). \"sections\" are phrases for the EPC's section search, which finds the pages in under a second (whole phrase: \"Oil Cooler\" finds \"Oil Cooler And Filter\", \"Oil Filter\" does not). \"pages\" are words the page name must then contain (any one phrase), e.g. front vs rear; they only say where to look, the base decides. \"prefer\" / \"avoid\" are words in a part's description, comments or features that settle a choice JLR leaves open. Confirm a change on real vehicles with `bin/jlr.js check <reg>...`; see jlr/README.md for the catalogues already checked. \"engine\": \"diesel\" marks a part only diesels have - when nothing matches and the pages it searched are for another engine (their names say \"Petrol\"), the line reads 'not on this vehicle' instead of 'missing'. \"require\" is strict: a part without those words is never quoted (wheel bearing: 1104 is a hub with bearing on some models, a bare hub on others). \"qty\" overrides the catalogue quantity (wheel bearings: one side, not the axle pair).",
